Inventor: Isolated Stratum-0 Time and Location Server — disclosure drafted.

Key architectural decisions captured in the document:

Core novelty over prior art:

    Hardware-enforced one-way Ethernet (data diode or transmit-only NIC) — no prior commercial GPS/NTP appliance does this at the physical layer
    GLONASS primary (not GPS), isolating from GPS-specific jamming campaigns
    Atomic holdover on GNSS anomaly — time accuracy degrades at the oscillator's free-running drift rate, not at the attacker's injected rate
    Broadcast-only NTP (no query-response stack) — clients receive time without issuing queries, mirroring local DNS resolver behavior

The NTPsec stripping question: Section 5.4 addresses this directly. If NTPsec's socket architecture can't be reduced to a pure transmit path, replace it with a minimal custom UDP/123 broadcast engine — trivial to implement, no inbound socket, kernel-level nftables drop rule as secondary backstop. That's the cleaner architecture anyway.

